-- [Ions, Nomenclature & Electrolytes] ans=D medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A student prepares four separate aqueous solutions, each at a concentration of \(0.10\ \text{M}\): glucose (\(\text{C}_6\text{H}_{12}\text{O}_6\)), acetic acid (\(\text{CH}_3\text{COOH}\)), sodium chloride (\(\text{NaCl}\)), and calcium chloride (\(\text{CaCl}_2\)). She places electrodes connected to a light bulb into each solution in turn. Which solution should make the bulb glow most brightly?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The glucose solution"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The acetic acid solution"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The sodium chloride solution"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The calcium chloride solution"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'D',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Ions in Solutions (GC).'' A solution conducts electricity only because it contains freely mobile charged particles, so brightness tracks the concentration of ions actually present. At equal molarity, the deciding factor is how many moles of mobile ions each dissolved formula unit releases: a strong electrolyte that dissociates completely into more ions per formula unit produces the greatest ion concentration and therefore the strongest conduction. (Choice D) is correct: \(\text{CaCl}_2\) is a soluble ionic compound that dissociates essentially completely into three ions per formula unit (\(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) plus two \(\text{Cl}^-\)), so a \(0.10\ \text{M}\) solution yields roughly \(0.30\ \text{M}\) total ions—more mobile charge carriers than any of the others—and makes the bulb glow most brightly. (Choice A) is incorrect: glucose is a molecular nonelectrolyte that dissolves as intact neutral molecules and produces no ions, so its solution cannot conduct and the bulb stays dark; assuming any dissolved solute conducts overlooks that conduction requires charged species. (Choice B) is incorrect: acetic acid is a weak electrolyte that only partially ionizes, so a \(0.10\ \text{M}\) solution contains only a small fraction of ions and conducts weakly; treating every acid as a strong conductor ignores partial ionization. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(\text{NaCl}\) is a strong electrolyte, but it dissociates into only two ions per formula unit (\(\text{Na}^+\) and \(\text{Cl}^-\)), giving about \(0.20\ \text{M}\) total ions—fewer mobile charges than \(\text{CaCl}_2\) at the same molarity, so it conducts well but not as strongly. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between electrolyte strength, the number of ions released per formula unit, and solution conductivity to rank four solutions rather than recall a single fact.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '50d17ea8-41bc-405b-9174-5ebb76aa92ef';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '50d17ea8-41bc-405b-9174-5ebb76aa92ef';
