  ('50d17ea8-41bc-405b-9174-5ebb76aa92ef', 'B', 'misconception', 'all acids are strong conductors'),
  ('50d17ea8-41bc-405b-9174-5ebb76aa92ef', 'C', 'misconception', 'all strong electrolytes conduct equally regardless of ion count');

-- [Types of Reactions] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Ammonia is produced industrially by the Haber process: \(\text{N}_2(g) + 3\,\text{H}_2(g) \rightarrow 2\,\text{NH}_3(g)\). A reaction vessel is charged with 28.0 g of \(\text{N}_2\) and 8.0 g of \(\text{H}_2\). Assuming the reaction goes to completion, what is the theoretical yield of \(\text{NH}_3\)? (Molar masses: \(\text{N}_2 = 28.0\ \text{g/mol}\), \(\text{H}_2 = 2.0\ \text{g/mol}\), \(\text{NH}_3 = 17.0\ \text{g/mol}\).)',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "34.0 g"}, {"label": "B", "text": "45.3 g"}, {"label": "C", "text": "17.0 g"}, {"label": "D", "text": "36.0 g"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Stoichiometry (GC).'' The theoretical yield is fixed by the limiting reactant, which is found by converting each reactant mass to moles and comparing that ratio to the stoichiometric ratio in the balanced equation. Here there are \(28.0\ \text{g} \div 28.0\ \text{g/mol} = 1.0\ \text{mol}\) \(\text{N}_2\) and \(8.0\ \text{g} \div 2.0\ \text{g/mol} = 4.0\ \text{mol}\) \(\text{H}_2\); the reaction needs 3 mol \(\text{H}_2\) per mol \(\text{N}_2\), so full consumption of 1.0 mol \(\text{N}_2\) would require only 3.0 mol \(\text{H}_2\), leaving \(\text{H}_2\) in excess and making \(\text{N}_2\) the limiting reactant. (Choice A) is correct: 1.0 mol \(\text{N}_2\) produces \(2 \times 1.0 = 2.0\ \text{mol}\) \(\text{NH}_3\), and \(2.0\ \text{mol} \times 17.0\ \text{g/mol} = 34.0\ \text{g}\). (Choice B) is incorrect: 45.3 g comes from treating \(\text{H}_2\) as limiting because its mass (8.0 g) is smaller than that of \(\text{N}_2\); mass cannot be compared directly, and on a molar basis \(\text{H}_2\) (4.0 mol) is actually the excess reactant. (Choice C) is incorrect: 17.0 g corresponds to 1.0 mol \(\text{NH}_3\), which drops the stoichiometric coefficient of 2 and incorrectly assumes 1 mol \(\text{N}_2\) yields only 1 mol \(\text{NH}_3\). (Choice D) is incorrect: 36.0 g is simply the sum of the two reactant masses (28.0 + 8.0), which misapplies conservation of mass by assuming all reactant mass ends up as \(\text{NH}_3\) even though \(\text{H}_2\) is left over. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the mole concept and the balanced equation to identify the limiting reactant and then calculate the theoretical mass of product it can generate.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '641ac692-1819-4b4c-833a-a86bd49bba38';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '641ac692-1819-4b4c-833a-a86bd49bba38';
