  ('641ac692-1819-4b4c-833a-a86bd49bba38', 'B', 'misconception', 'compares reactant masses instead of moles to find limiting reactant'),
  ('641ac692-1819-4b4c-833a-a86bd49bba38', 'C', 'misconception', 'ignores stoichiometric coefficient when scaling limiting reactant to product'),
  ('641ac692-1819-4b4c-833a-a86bd49bba38', 'D', 'misconception', 'misapplies conservation of mass by summing all reactant masses as product');

-- [Ions, Nomenclature & Electrolytes] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A student prepares three aqueous solutions, each 0.10 M, from covalent (molecular) compounds and places each between electrodes wired to a light bulb. The hydrogen chloride \(\text{(HCl)}\) solution lights the bulb brightly, the acetic acid \(\text{(CH}_3\text{COOH)}\) solution lights it only dimly, and the glucose \(\text{(C}_6\text{H}_{12}\text{O}_6\text{)}\) solution leaves the bulb dark. Which statement best explains the pattern of conductivity observed across the three solutions?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Conductivity tracks the concentration of mobile ions each solute produces, which is high for HCl, low for acetic acid, and negligible for glucose."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Conductivity tracks the number of covalent bonds broken by the solvent, which is largest for HCl, smaller for acetic acid, and zero for glucose."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Conductivity tracks the molar mass of each dissolved solute, which is smallest for HCl, larger for acetic acid, and largest for glucose."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Conductivity tracks the number of intact solute molecules dispersed in solution, which is greatest for HCl and least for glucose."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Ions in Solutions (GC).'' A substance conducts electricity only if it contains freely mobile charged particles, and in solution those carriers are dissolved ions; the more completely a solute yields ions, the greater the conductivity, so conductivity reflects the concentration of ions actually present, not the identity or bonding of the starting compound. HCl reacts essentially completely with water to give \(\text{H}_3\text{O}^{+}\) and \(\text{Cl}^{-}\), making it a strong electrolyte (bright bulb); acetic acid ionizes only slightly, generating a low ion concentration as a weak electrolyte (dim bulb); glucose dissolves as intact neutral molecules and yields no ions, so it is a nonelectrolyte (dark bulb). (Choice A) is correct: it correctly identifies mobile-ion concentration as the quantity that determines conductivity and matches the three cases (high, low, negligible) to the observed brightness. (Choice B) is incorrect: what matters is how many ions end up in solution, not how many bonds break; glucose molecules do dissolve and interact with water, yet the process produces no ions, so bond-breaking is not the operative variable. (Choice C) is incorrect: conductivity does not depend on molar mass, and the trend fails anyway because a heavier solute can be a stronger electrolyte; the light bulb responds to ion concentration, not to solute mass. (Choice D) is incorrect: it is the ions produced, not the intact neutral molecules, that carry current, so a solution rich in undissociated molecules (like glucose) conducts poorly rather than well. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that solution conductivity is governed by the concentration of mobile ions to rank three covalent solutes by their degree of ionization.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '3adc4c30-6a1e-43d5-9aa0-c9dc8dacec16';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '3adc4c30-6a1e-43d5-9aa0-c9dc8dacec16';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('3adc4c30-6a1e-43d5-9aa0-c9dc8dacec16', 'B', 'misconception', 'conflating bond-breaking with ion production'),
  ('3adc4c30-6a1e-43d5-9aa0-c9dc8dacec16', 'C', 'partial_truth', 'invoking molar mass as the causal variable'),
  ('3adc4c30-6a1e-43d5-9aa0-c9dc8dacec16', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'attributing conduction to neutral molecules instead of ions');

-- [Types of Reactions] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'When chlorine gas is dissolved in cold water, it reacts according to \(\text{Cl}_2(g) + \text{H}_2\text{O}(l) \rightarrow \text{HClO}(aq) + \text{HCl}(aq)\). Which statement correctly describes what happens to the oxidation number of chlorine in this reaction?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Chlorine is both oxidized and reduced, moving from 0 in \\(\\text{Cl}_2\\) to \\(+1\\) in \\(\\text{HClO}\\) and to \\(-1\\) in \\(\\text{HCl}\\)."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Chlorine is only oxidized, moving from 0 in \\(\\text{Cl}_2\\) to \\(+1\\) in \\(\\text{HClO}\\) while staying at 0 in \\(\\text{HCl}\\)."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Chlorine is only reduced, moving from 0 in \\(\\text{Cl}_2\\) to \\(-1\\) in \\(\\text{HCl}\\) while staying at 0 in \\(\\text{HClO}\\)."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Chlorine is neither oxidized nor reduced, remaining at 0 in both \\(\\text{HClO}\\) and \\(\\text{HCl}\\)."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Stoichiometry (GC).'' Oxidation numbers are assigned by the rule that an element in its elemental form is 0, and that within a compound the oxidation numbers must sum to the overall charge (with H taken as \(+1\) and O as \(-2\)). In \(\text{Cl}_2\), chlorine is elemental, so its oxidation number is 0; in \(\text{HClO}\), \(+1\) for H and \(-2\) for O force chlorine to \(+1\); in \(\text{HCl}\), \(+1\) for H forces chlorine to \(-1\). Because one element is simultaneously oxidized and reduced, this is a disproportionation reaction. (Choice A) is correct: chlorine starts at 0 and ends at two different oxidation numbers, \(+1\) in \(\text{HClO}\) (oxidation, an increase) and \(-1\) in \(\text{HCl}\) (reduction, a decrease), so the same element serves as both oxidant and reductant. (Choice B) is incorrect: it accounts for the chlorine that becomes \(+1\) in \(\text{HClO}\) but wrongly assigns 0 to chlorine in \(\text{HCl}\); applying the rules, H is \(+1\) so chlorine must be \(-1\), meaning reduction also occurs. (Choice C) is incorrect: it captures the reduction to \(-1\) in \(\text{HCl}\) but wrongly leaves chlorine at 0 in \(\text{HClO}\); with O at \(-2\) and H at \(+1\), chlorine must be \(+1\) there, so oxidation also occurs. (Choice D) is incorrect: chlorine is elemental (0) only in \(\text{Cl}_2\), but in the products the surrounding atoms force chlorine to \(+1\) and \(-1\), so its oxidation number does change and a redox process takes place. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the oxidation-number rules to each species and infer that a single element being both oxidized and reduced defines a disproportionation reaction.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '7f327792-4884-48a9-828b-89ce8ecc6578';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '7f327792-4884-48a9-828b-89ce8ecc6578';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('7f327792-4884-48a9-828b-89ce8ecc6578', 'B', 'misconception', 'partial redox analysis — notices oxidation to +1 but misses the simultaneous reduction'),
  ('7f327792-4884-48a9-828b-89ce8ecc6578', 'C', 'misconception', 'partial redox analysis — notices reduction to -1 but misses the simultaneous oxidation'),
  ('7f327792-4884-48a9-828b-89ce8ecc6578', 'D', 'misconception', 'assumes an element appearing on both sides undergoes no oxidation-state change');

-- [Empirical & Molecular Formula + Percent Composition] ans=D medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Combustion analysis of an unknown organic acid shows it to be \(40.0\%\) carbon, \(6.7\%\) hydrogen, and \(53.3\%\) oxygen by mass. A separate mass-spectrometry measurement gives its molar mass as \(90\,\text{g/mol}\). What is the molecular formula of the compound?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(\\text{CH}_2\\text{O}\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_4\\text{O}_2\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(\\text{C}_3\\text{H}_8\\text{O}_3\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(\\text{C}_3\\text{H}_6\\text{O}_3\\)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'D',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Stoichiometry (GC).'' A molecular formula is found by first converting percent composition to an empirical formula, then scaling that empirical unit by the whole-number multiplier \(n\) equal to the molar mass divided by the empirical-formula mass. Treating a 100-g sample, the masses in grams equal the percentages, so the moles are \(40.0/12.01 = 3.33\) mol C, \(6.7/1.008 = 6.65\) mol H, and \(53.3/16.00 = 3.33\) mol O; dividing by the smallest gives a 1:2:1 ratio and the empirical formula \(\text{CH}_2\text{O}\) (empirical-formula mass \(\approx 30\,\text{g/mol}\)). (Choice D) is correct: the multiplier is \(n = 90/30 = 3\), so multiplying every subscript in \(\text{CH}_2\text{O}\) by 3 gives \(\text{C}_3\text{H}_6\text{O}_3\) (molar mass \(\approx 90\,\text{g/mol}\), matching the measurement). (Choice A) is incorrect: \(\text{CH}_2\text{O}\) is only the empirical formula; it fits the percent data but has a mass of \(30\,\text{g/mol}\), one-third of the measured \(90\,\text{g/mol}\), so the molar-mass scaling step was skipped. (Choice B) is incorrect: \(\text{C}_2\text{H}_4\text{O}_2\) applies a multiplier of 2, corresponding to a molar mass of \(60\,\text{g/mol}\) rather than the measured \(90\,\text{g/mol}\). (Choice C) is incorrect: \(\text{C}_3\text{H}_8\text{O}_3\) scales carbon and oxygen correctly but distorts the hydrogen ratio, giving a mass of \(92\,\text{g/mol}\) and an H:C ratio that does not match the \(6.7\%\) hydrogen data. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply percent composition, mole ratios, and empirical-to-molecular scaling in sequence to derive the formula from data the stem does not hand you.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '491422e1-c336-4de5-91d7-fc8a55226018';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '491422e1-c336-4de5-91d7-fc8a55226018';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('491422e1-c336-4de5-91d7-fc8a55226018', 'A', 'misconception', 'stops_at_empirical_formula'),
  ('491422e1-c336-4de5-91d7-fc8a55226018', 'B', 'misconception', 'wrong_scaling_multiplier'),
  ('491422e1-c336-4de5-91d7-fc8a55226018', 'C', 'misconception', 'incorrect_hydrogen_ratio');

-- [Limiting Reagent, Theoretical & Percent Yield] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A chemist combines \(28.0\ \text{g}\) of \(\text{N}_2\) with \(10.0\ \text{g}\) of \(\text{H}_2\) and runs the synthesis \(\text{N}_2 + 3\,\text{H}_2 \rightarrow 2\,\text{NH}_3\), isolating \(24.0\ \text{g}\) of \(\text{NH}_3\). Using molar masses \(\text{N}_2 = 28.0\), \(\text{H}_2 = 2.0\), and \(\text{NH}_3 = 17.0\ \text{g/mol}\), what is the percent yield?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "71%"}, {"label": "B", "text": "43%"}, {"label": "C", "text": "86%"}, {"label": "D", "text": "35%"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Stoichiometry (GC).'' Percent yield is the actual yield divided by the theoretical yield times 100%, and the theoretical yield is the maximum product the limiting reactant can form; the limiting reactant must be found first by comparing supplied mole ratios to the balanced equation. Here \(28.0\ \text{g}\ \text{N}_2 = 1.00\ \text{mol}\) and \(10.0\ \text{g}\ \text{H}_2 = 5.0\ \text{mol}\); reacting all \(1.00\ \text{mol}\ \text{N}_2\) needs \(3.00\ \text{mol}\ \text{H}_2\), so \(\text{H}_2\) is in excess and \(\text{N}_2\) is limiting. (Choice A) is correct: complete reaction of \(1.00\ \text{mol}\ \text{N}_2\) gives \(2.00\ \text{mol}\ \text{NH}_3 = 34.0\ \text{g}\) theoretical, so percent yield \(= \frac{24.0}{34.0}\times100\% \approx 71\%\). (Choice B) is incorrect: 43% comes from treating \(\text{H}_2\) as limiting (or assuming all \(5.0\ \text{mol}\ \text{H}_2\) reacts), which predicts \(3.33\ \text{mol}\ \text{NH}_3 = 56.7\ \text{g}\) and \(\frac{24.0}{56.7}\times100\% \approx 43\%\); the excess reactant never sets the theoretical yield. (Choice C) is incorrect: 86% comes from dividing the actual mass by the supplied mass of the limiting reactant, \(\frac{24.0}{28.0}\times100\%\), skipping the mole-to-mole conversion through the \(1{:}2\) \(\text{N}_2{:}\text{NH}_3\) ratio. (Choice D) is incorrect: 35% comes from using the atomic mass \(14.0\ \text{g/mol}\) instead of the diatomic \(28.0\ \text{g/mol}\) for \(\text{N}_2\), which doubles the moles of \(\text{N}_2\) to \(2.00\ \text{mol}\) and inflates the theoretical yield to \(68.0\ \text{g}\). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply limiting-reactant identification and mole-ratio stoichiometry to novel masses to derive the theoretical yield and then the percent yield.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'ccfce1d0-9f50-44f6-857c-c324c96e6f3d';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'ccfce1d0-9f50-44f6-857c-c324c96e6f3d';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('ccfce1d0-9f50-44f6-857c-c324c96e6f3d', 'B', 'misconception', 'wrong-limiting-reactant'),
  ('ccfce1d0-9f50-44f6-857c-c324c96e6f3d', 'C', 'misconception', 'skips-mole-ratio-conversion'),
  ('ccfce1d0-9f50-44f6-857c-c324c96e6f3d', 'D', 'misconception', 'atomic-vs-molecular-mass-error');

-- [Ions, Nomenclature & Electrolytes] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A chemist prepares four aqueous solutions, each at the same concentration of \(0.10\ \text{M}\): iron(III) chloride \(\text{FeCl}_3\), magnesium chloride \(\text{MgCl}_2\), sodium chloride \(\text{NaCl}\), and glucose \(\text{C}_6\text{H}_{12}\text{O}_6\). Assuming each solute is fully soluble and any ionic dissociation goes to completion, which solution has the highest electrical conductivity?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The \\(\\text{FeCl}_3\\) solution"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The \\(\\text{MgCl}_2\\) solution"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The \\(\\text{NaCl}\\) solution"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The \\(\\text{C}_6\\text{H}_{12}\\text{O}_6\\) solution"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Ions in Solutions (GC).'' The conductivity of an aqueous solution rises with the concentration of freely mobile ions it contains: to conduct current, a solution must hold dissolved, charged species, and more charge carriers per liter means greater conductivity. Because all four solutes are at the same \(0.10\ \text{M}\) formula-unit concentration, the deciding factor is how many mobile ions each formula unit releases upon complete dissociation. (Choice A) is correct: \(\text{FeCl}_3\) dissociates into \(\text{Fe}^{3+}\) plus \(3\,\text{Cl}^{-}\), yielding 4 ions per formula unit, so \(0.10\ \text{M}\ \text{FeCl}_3\) produces \(0.40\ \text{M}\) total ions — the largest concentration of charge carriers and therefore the highest conductivity. (Choice B) is incorrect: \(\text{MgCl}_2\) gives \(\text{Mg}^{2+}\) plus \(2\,\text{Cl}^{-}\), only 3 ions per formula unit (\(0.30\ \text{M}\) ions); the higher \(2+\) charge on \(\text{Mg}^{2+}\) does not compensate, because conductivity here tracks the number of mobile ions produced, and \(\text{MgCl}_2\) produces fewer than \(\text{FeCl}_3\). (Choice C) is incorrect: \(\text{NaCl}\) is the most familiar strong electrolyte, but it releases only \(\text{Na}^{+}\) and \(\text{Cl}^{-}\), 2 ions per formula unit (\(0.20\ \text{M}\) ions), so at equal molarity it conducts less than the salts that liberate more ions. (Choice D) is incorrect: glucose is a nonelectrolyte that dissolves as intact neutral molecules and releases no ions at all, so its solution has essentially no charge carriers and the lowest conductivity, not the highest. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between conductivity and mobile-ion concentration to compare solutes that differ in how many ions each formula unit releases.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '6766e542-e22a-46e3-aa30-055457a88811';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '6766e542-e22a-46e3-aa30-055457a88811';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('6766e542-e22a-46e3-aa30-055457a88811', 'B', 'misconception', 'charge_magnitude_over_ion_count'),
  ('6766e542-e22a-46e3-aa30-055457a88811', 'C', 'misconception', 'most_familiar_strong_electrolyte'),
  ('6766e542-e22a-46e3-aa30-055457a88811', 'D', 'misconception', 'dissolves_therefore_conducts');

-- [Ions, Nomenclature & Electrolytes] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Aluminum sulfate is an ionic compound built from the aluminum cation \(\text{Al}^{3+}\) and the sulfate anion \(\text{SO}_4^{2-}\). For the neutral solid, which formula correctly represents the ratio in which these two ions combine?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(\\text{Al}_2(\\text{SO}_4)_3\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(\\text{AlSO}_4\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(\\text{Al}_3(\\text{SO}_4)_2\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(\\text{Al}_2\\text{SO}_4\\)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Ions in Solutions (GC).'' An ionic compound must be electrically neutral, so the total positive charge contributed by the cations must exactly cancel the total negative charge contributed by the anions; the subscripts in the formula are the smallest whole-number ratio of ions that achieves this balance. Here the aluminum ion carries a \(3+\) charge and the polyatomic sulfate ion carries a \(2-\) charge, so the ratio must reconcile a \(3+\) with a \(2-\). (Choice A) is correct: the least common multiple of \(3\) and \(2\) is \(6\), so two \(\text{Al}^{3+}\) ions supply \(6+\) and three \(\text{SO}_4^{2-}\) ions supply \(6-\), giving the neutral \(2:3\) ratio written as \(\text{Al}_2(\text{SO}_4)_3\). (Choice B) is incorrect: \(\text{AlSO}_4\) pairs the ions \(1:1\), which leaves a net charge of \((3+)+(2-)=1+\); this is the error of assuming every cation and anion combine one-to-one without checking the magnitudes of their charges. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(\text{Al}_3(\text{SO}_4)_2\) results from using each ion''s own charge magnitude as its own subscript — putting the aluminum charge of \(3\) on aluminum and the sulfate charge of \(2\) on sulfate instead of crossing the charges over; this gives \(3(3+)=9+\) against \(2(2-)=4-\), a net \(5+\) rather than a neutral compound. (Choice D) is incorrect: \(\text{Al}_2\text{SO}_4\) balances two \(\text{Al}^{3+}\) ions \((6+)\) against a single sulfate treated as \(6-\); it reflects a misremembered sulfate charge of \(3-\) (or a failure to include the three sulfate units needed) and is not neutral for the actual \(2-\) sulfate ion. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle of charge neutrality, together with the known charges of the aluminum and sulfate ions, to derive the correct whole-number combining ratio rather than recall a memorized formula.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'ccba48c1-a286-4950-a372-ba134d8e4336';
