-- [Ions, Nomenclature & Electrolytes] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A student places electrodes connected to a light bulb into four separate beakers, each holding a 0.10 M aqueous solution of a different solute. The bulb glows brightly in three of the beakers but only dimly in the fourth, indicating that this solute produces a comparatively low concentration of mobile ions. Which solute is in the beaker where the bulb glows dimly?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Acetic acid, \\(\\text{CH}_3\\text{COOH}\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Sodium chloride, \\(\\text{NaCl}\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Hydrogen chloride, \\(\\text{HCl}\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Potassium nitrate, \\(\\text{KNO}_3\\)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Ions in Solutions (GC).'' The brightness of the bulb tracks the concentration of mobile ions in solution: a strong electrolyte ionizes essentially completely and conducts well (bright), whereas a weak electrolyte ionizes only partially and produces a low ion concentration (dim). Because all four solutions are at the same 0.10 M concentration, the difference in brightness must come from how completely each solute dissociates, not from how much was dissolved. (Choice A) is correct: acetic acid is a weak acid that reacts only partially with water, so at 0.10 M just a small fraction of its molecules ionize into \(\text{CH}_3\text{COO}^-\) and \(\text{H}_3\text{O}^+\); the resulting low ion concentration makes it a weak electrolyte and produces only a dim glow. (Choice B) is incorrect: sodium chloride is an ionic compound, and ionic compounds dissociate nearly completely when dissolved, making \(\text{NaCl}\) a strong electrolyte that yields a high ion concentration and a bright bulb. (Choice C) is incorrect: hydrogen chloride is molecular, but in water it reacts essentially 100% as a strong acid, transferring \(\text{H}^+\) to water to give \(\text{H}_3\text{O}^+\) and \(\text{Cl}^-\); this makes it a strong electrolyte with high conductivity, not a dim one. (Choice D) is incorrect: potassium nitrate is a soluble ionic compound that dissociates fully into \(\text{K}^+\) and \(\text{NO}_3^-\), so it is a strong electrolyte and the bulb glows brightly. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between degree of ionization and solution conductivity to classify each solute as a strong or weak electrolyte at equal concentration.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'e7a3b517-2d4d-443a-b98c-e3eb478e9269';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'e7a3b517-2d4d-443a-b98c-e3eb478e9269';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('e7a3b517-2d4d-443a-b98c-e3eb478e9269', 'B', 'misconception', 'confuses ionic dissolution with partial ionization'),
  ('e7a3b517-2d4d-443a-b98c-e3eb478e9269', 'C', 'misconception', 'assumes a molecular/covalent solute must be a weak electrolyte'),
  ('e7a3b517-2d4d-443a-b98c-e3eb478e9269', 'D', 'misconception', 'treats a soluble ionic salt as a weak conductor');

-- [Ions, Nomenclature & Electrolytes] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A student memorizes that the "-ate" suffix names the reference oxyanion of a series and that "per-" and "hypo-" shift the oxygen count up and down from there. Reasoning that all "-ate" ions should therefore behave alike, the student predicts that the sulfate ion and the chlorate ion carry the same number of oxygen atoms and the same charge. In fact, sulfate is \(\text{SO}_4^{2-}\) and chlorate is \(\text{ClO}_3^{-}\). Which formula correctly represents the phosphate ion, the "-ate" oxyanion of phosphorus?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(\\text{PO}_4^{3-}\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(\\text{PO}_3^{-}\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(\\text{PO}_4^{2-}\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(\\text{PO}_4^{-}\\)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Ions in Solutions (GC).'' The naming system for oxyanions (per-, -ate, -ite, hypo-) fixes only the relative ordering of oxygen atoms within a single element''s series; it does not set a universal oxygen count or a universal charge, because those depend on the central atom. As the text notes, the number of oxygens tied to a given suffix is not consistent across elements—nitrate is \(\text{NO}_3^{-}\) while sulfate is \(\text{SO}_4^{2-}\)—so each familiar ion''s formula and charge must be known. Phosphate, the "-ate" ion of phosphorus, is \(\text{PO}_4^{3-}\). (Choice A) is correct: phosphate carries four oxygen atoms and a 3- charge, the value the AAMC lists among the familiar ions to know; nothing in the stem''s sulfate or chlorate data supplies this, so it must be recalled and applied. (Choice B) is incorrect: \(\text{PO}_3^{-}\) copies chlorate''s three-oxygen, 1- pattern onto phosphorus, the exact "all -ate ions match" error the stem flags; phosphate is not the phosphorus analog of chlorate. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(\text{PO}_4^{2-}\) borrows sulfate''s 2- charge while keeping four oxygens, mixing a correct oxygen count with the wrong charge for the phosphorus center. (Choice D) is incorrect: \(\text{PO}_4^{-}\) keeps four oxygens but assigns a 1- charge, treating every "-ate" ion as singly charged the way chlorate and nitrate are. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that oxyanion suffixes fix only relative oxygen ordering—not absolute oxygen count or charge—and then recall the correct formula and charge of a familiar ion whose value the stem never provides.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'ce88e2db-676d-4e25-985b-94e2ebde1317';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'ce88e2db-676d-4e25-985b-94e2ebde1317';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('ce88e2db-676d-4e25-985b-94e2ebde1317', 'B', 'misconception', 'assumes a suffix maps to a fixed oxygen count/charge across all elements'),
  ('ce88e2db-676d-4e25-985b-94e2ebde1317', 'C', 'misconception', 'transfers the charge of one -ate ion (sulfate 2-) onto a different central atom'),
  ('ce88e2db-676d-4e25-985b-94e2ebde1317', 'D', 'misconception', 'treats every -ate oxyanion as singly charged like chlorate/nitrate');

-- [Ions, Nomenclature & Electrolytes] ans=C medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A student prepares four separate aqueous solutions, each at a concentration of 0.10 M, and tests the electrical conductivity of each by measuring how brightly a bulb glows in a circuit. Assuming complete dissociation of ionic solutes, which solution should conduct electricity most strongly?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "0.10 M \\(\\text{NaCl}\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "0.10 M \\(\\text{CH}_3\\text{COOH}\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "0.10 M \\(\\text{CaCl}_2\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "0.10 M glucose \\((\\text{C}_6\\text{H}_{12}\\text{O}_6)\\)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Ions in Solutions (GC).'' The conductivity of a solution depends on the concentration of freely mobile ions: strong electrolytes dissociate essentially completely, weak electrolytes ionize only partially, and nonelectrolytes produce no ions at all. Because the solutions are at equal molarity, the best conductor is the one whose solute is a strong electrolyte AND releases the most ions per formula unit. (Choice C) is correct: \(\text{CaCl}_2\) is a strong electrolyte that dissociates completely into three ions per formula unit \((\text{Ca}^{2+} + 2\,\text{Cl}^-)\), giving \(0.30\) M total ions—the highest mobile-ion concentration of the four solutions—so it conducts most strongly. (Choice A) is incorrect: \(\text{NaCl}\) is also a strong electrolyte, but it dissociates into only two ions per formula unit \((\text{Na}^+ + \text{Cl}^-)\), yielding \(0.20\) M total ions, fewer than \(\text{CaCl}_2\) at the same molarity. (Choice B) is incorrect: acetic acid \((\text{CH}_3\text{COOH})\) is a weak electrolyte that only partially ionizes, so a 0.10 M solution contains far less than 0.10 M ions and conducts poorly despite being an acid. (Choice D) is incorrect: glucose is a molecular sugar and a nonelectrolyte; it dissolves without producing any ions, so its solution cannot conduct electricity. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between electrolyte strength, ions produced per formula unit, and solution conductivity to compare four equimolar solutions.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '2cee49fe-94cc-48a0-92ec-e6fdbf6b10c3';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '2cee49fe-94cc-48a0-92ec-e6fdbf6b10c3';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('2cee49fe-94cc-48a0-92ec-e6fdbf6b10c3', 'A', 'misconception', 'picks the familiar strong electrolyte but ignores ions-per-formula-unit'),
  ('2cee49fe-94cc-48a0-92ec-e6fdbf6b10c3', 'B', 'misconception', 'assumes any acid is a strong conductor, forgetting weak-electrolyte partial ionization'),
  ('2cee49fe-94cc-48a0-92ec-e6fdbf6b10c3', 'D', 'misconception', 'assumes any dissolved solute conducts, forgetting molecular sugars are nonelectrolytes');

-- [Types of Reactions] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A student mixes a clear solution of \(\text{Ba(NO}_3)_2\) with a clear solution of \(\text{Na}_2\text{SO}_4\), and a white solid immediately settles out. Which species is the precipitate?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(\\text{NaNO}_3\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(\\text{BaSO}_4\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(\\text{Ba(OH)}_2\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(\\text{Na}_2\\text{SO}_4\\)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Stoichiometry (GC).'' In a double-displacement (metathesis) reaction, the cations and anions of the two dissolved salts swap partners, and solubility rules then determine which of the two possible product salts is insoluble and drops out as a precipitate: \(\text{Ba(NO}_3)_2(aq) + \text{Na}_2\text{SO}_4(aq) \rightarrow \text{BaSO}_4(s) + 2\,\text{NaNO}_3(aq)\). (Choice B) is correct: swapping partners gives \(\text{BaSO}_4\) and \(\text{NaNO}_3\); although most sulfates are soluble, sulfates of \(\text{Ba}^{2+}\) (along with \(\text{Ag}^+\), \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\), \(\text{Hg}_2^{2+}\), \(\text{Pb}^{2+}\), and \(\text{Sr}^{2+}\)) are a listed exception, so \(\text{BaSO}_4\) is the insoluble solid. (Choice A) is incorrect: \(\text{NaNO}_3\) is the other partner-swap product, but every nitrate salt and every group-1 (e.g., \(\text{Na}^+\)) salt is soluble, so it stays fully dissolved as spectator ions rather than precipitating. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(\text{Ba(OH)}_2\) invents a hydroxide ion that is not present—neither starting solution supplies \(\text{OH}^-\), so this compound cannot form from the ions available. (Choice D) is incorrect: \(\text{Na}_2\text{SO}_4\) is one of the starting reactants, present as dissolved \(\text{Na}^+\) and \(\text{SO}_4^{2-}\); it is soluble and was already in solution, so it is not the newly formed solid. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the partner-swap pattern of metathesis reactions together with the solubility-rule exception for barium sulfate to a specific pair of reactants.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '1bbcf70a-0398-4311-b926-983d61a003d3';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '1bbcf70a-0398-4311-b926-983d61a003d3';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('1bbcf70a-0398-4311-b926-983d61a003d3', 'A', 'misconception', 'picks the soluble spectator product as the precipitate'),
  ('1bbcf70a-0398-4311-b926-983d61a003d3', 'C', 'misconception', 'invents an ion (OH-) not present in solution'),
  ('1bbcf70a-0398-4311-b926-983d61a003d3', 'D', 'misconception', 'identifies an unreacted starting reactant as the solid');

-- [Types of Reactions] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Iron shavings dropped into a blue copper(II) sulfate solution react according to \(\text{Fe(s)} + \text{Cu}^{2+}\text{(aq)} \rightarrow \text{Fe}^{2+}\text{(aq)} + \text{Cu(s)}\), and a reddish copper coating appears on the iron while the blue color fades. Which species in this reaction acts as the oxidizing agent?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(\\text{Cu}^{2+}\\text{(aq)}\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(\\text{Fe(s)}\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(\\text{Cu(s)}\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(\\text{Fe}^{2+}\\text{(aq)}\\)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Stoichiometry (GC).'' An oxidizing agent (oxidant) is the substance that brings about the oxidation of another species and, in doing so, is itself reduced — its oxidation number decreases. To find it, assign oxidation numbers and locate the reactant whose number drops. Here \(\text{Fe}\) goes from 0 in the free metal to +2 in \(\text{Fe}^{2+}\) (oxidized, oxidation number increases), while the copper in \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\) goes from +2 to 0 in \(\text{Cu(s)}\) (reduced, oxidation number decreases). (Choice A) is correct: \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\text{(aq)}\) is the reactant species whose oxidation number decreases from +2 to 0, so it is reduced; because it accepts the electrons lost by iron and thereby oxidizes the iron, \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\) is the oxidizing agent. (Choice B) is incorrect: \(\text{Fe(s)}\) is oxidized (0 to +2), meaning it loses electrons and brings about the reduction of copper; a species that is itself oxidized is the reducing agent, not the oxidizing agent — this is the reversed-role trap. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(\text{Cu(s)}\) is the reduced product, the outcome of \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\) gaining electrons; the oxidizing agent is the reactant that does the oxidizing, not the elemental product that is formed after reduction is complete. (Choice D) is incorrect: \(\text{Fe}^{2+}\text{(aq)}\) is the oxidized product formed when iron loses electrons; it is a product of oxidation rather than a reactant that drives it, so it cannot be the oxidizing agent in this forward reaction. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply oxidation-number assignments to identify which reactant is reduced and match it to the definition of an oxidizing agent.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'f441bb96-f246-4989-88d9-6ee3cf53f0b5';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'f441bb96-f246-4989-88d9-6ee3cf53f0b5';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('f441bb96-f246-4989-88d9-6ee3cf53f0b5', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'oxidizing-agent/reducing-agent role reversal'),
  ('f441bb96-f246-4989-88d9-6ee3cf53f0b5', 'C', 'misconception', 'confuses reduced product with the oxidant reactant'),
  ('f441bb96-f246-4989-88d9-6ee3cf53f0b5', 'D', 'misconception', 'confuses oxidized product with an agent');

-- [Ions, Nomenclature & Electrolytes] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A student prepares four separate aqueous solutions, each at the same concentration of \(0.10\ \text{M}\), and tests each by immersing electrodes connected to a light bulb. The solutes are glucose (\(\text{C}_6\text{H}_{12}\text{O}_6\)), acetic acid (\(\text{CH}_3\text{COOH}\)), sodium chloride (\(\text{NaCl}\)), and calcium chloride (\(\text{CaCl}_2\)). Which solution would make the bulb glow most brightly?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The calcium chloride solution"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The sodium chloride solution"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The acetic acid solution"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The glucose solution"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Ions in Solutions (GC).'' The brightness of the bulb reflects electrical conductivity, which depends on the concentration of freely mobile ions in solution; because all four solutes are at the same molarity, the solution that dissociates into the greatest number of ions per formula unit will carry the most current. Strong electrolytes dissociate essentially completely, weak electrolytes ionize only slightly, and nonelectrolytes produce no ions at all. (Choice A) is correct: calcium chloride is a strong electrolyte that dissociates completely into \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) and \(2\,\text{Cl}^-\), yielding three moles of ions per mole of solute; at equal molarity this gives the highest concentration of mobile ions and therefore the brightest bulb. (Choice B) is incorrect: sodium chloride is also a strong electrolyte, but it dissociates into only \(\text{Na}^+\) and \(\text{Cl}^-\)—two ions per formula unit—so at the same concentration it produces fewer mobile ions than \(\text{CaCl}_2\) and conducts less well. (Choice C) is incorrect: acetic acid is a weak acid and thus a weak electrolyte, ionizing only about 1% in water, so a \(0.10\ \text{M}\) solution contains a very low concentration of ions and conducts only weakly despite being an acid. (Choice D) is incorrect: glucose is a molecular nonelectrolyte that dissolves without producing any ions, so its solution cannot conduct electricity and the bulb would not glow. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between electrolyte strength, ion count per formula unit, and solution conductivity to rank four equal-concentration solutions.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '58f52d27-a8b0-42be-8881-887bfea39745';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '58f52d27-a8b0-42be-8881-887bfea39745';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('58f52d27-a8b0-42be-8881-887bfea39745', 'B', 'adjacent_fact', 'correct-category-wrong-magnitude'),
  ('58f52d27-a8b0-42be-8881-887bfea39745', 'C', 'misconception', 'acid-equals-strong-conductor'),
  ('58f52d27-a8b0-42be-8881-887bfea39745', 'D', 'misconception', 'any-dissolved-solute-conducts');

-- [Molecules, Moles & Molar Mass] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'The sum of the atomic weights of the atoms in a chemical formula can be called the "molecular weight" only for substances that exist as discrete molecules; for all other substances the correct term is "formula weight." For which of the following compounds is "molecular weight" the appropriate term?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Dinitrogen tetroxide, \\(\\text{N}_2\\text{O}_4\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Potassium sulfate, \\(\\text{K}_2\\text{SO}_4\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Calcium fluoride, \\(\\text{CaF}_2\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Ammonium chloride, \\(\\text{NH}_4\\text{Cl}\\)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Stoichiometry (GC).'' The term molecular weight (molecular mass) may correctly be applied only when the chemical formula represents a real, discrete molecule, which occurs for covalent compounds built from nonmetals; for ionic compounds the formula gives only the smallest whole-number ratio of ions in an extended lattice, so summing the atomic weights yields a formula weight rather than a molecular weight. Deciding which term fits therefore requires classifying each compound as covalent or ionic, not memorizing a name. (Choice A) is correct: dinitrogen tetroxide is composed only of the nonmetals nitrogen and oxygen, so it is a covalent substance that exists as individual \(\text{N}_2\text{O}_4\) molecules; summing the atomic weights describes an actual particle, so molecular weight is the appropriate term. (Choice B) is incorrect: potassium sulfate pairs the metal potassium with the sulfate anion, making it an ionic compound with no discrete molecules, so its summed atomic weights constitute a formula weight even though the formula looks molecular. (Choice C) is incorrect: calcium fluoride combines the metal calcium with fluorine as \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) and \(\text{F}^{-}\) ions in a continuous lattice; a three-atom formula unit is not a molecule, so the correct term is formula weight. (Choice D) is incorrect: although the ammonium ion contains covalent N-H bonds, the compound as a whole is ionic, built from \(\text{NH}_4^{+}\) and \(\text{Cl}^{-}\) ions rather than discrete molecules, so its summed atomic weights give a formula weight. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the covalent-versus-ionic distinction to each compound to determine whether a discrete molecule exists and therefore which mass term is correct.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '308dee93-9c82-41b5-9da9-665d21f53fd3';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '308dee93-9c82-41b5-9da9-665d21f53fd3';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('308dee93-9c82-41b5-9da9-665d21f53fd3', 'B', 'misconception', 'treats any definite chemical formula as representing a molecule, ignoring ionic bonding'),
  ('308dee93-9c82-41b5-9da9-665d21f53fd3', 'C', 'misconception', 'assumes a small fixed-atom-count formula unit is a molecule'),
  ('308dee93-9c82-41b5-9da9-665d21f53fd3', 'D', 'misconception', 'concludes the whole compound is molecular because a polyatomic ion contains covalent bonds');

-- [Types of Reactions] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In a disproportionation reaction, a single element is simultaneously oxidized and reduced. Which of the following balanced equations represents a disproportionation reaction?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(3\\,\\text{Cl}_2 + 6\\,\\text{OH}^- \\rightarrow 5\\,\\text{Cl}^- + \\text{ClO}_3^- + 3\\,\\text{H}_2\\text{O}\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(2\\,\\text{Al} + 3\\,\\text{Br}_2 \\rightarrow 2\\,\\text{AlBr}_3\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(\\text{Zn} + \\text{CuSO}_4 \\rightarrow \\text{ZnSO}_4 + \\text{Cu}\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(\\text{CaCO}_3 \\rightarrow \\text{CaO} + \\text{CO}_2\\)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Stoichiometry (GC).'' A disproportionation reaction is a redox process in which the same element functions as both the oxidant and the reductant, so one element''s oxidation number must both increase and decrease within the single equation. To identify one, you assign oxidation numbers to each element on both sides and look for a single element whose oxidation number splits in two directions. (Choice A) is correct: chlorine begins as \(\text{Cl}_2\) with an oxidation number of \(0\); some chlorine is reduced to \(\text{Cl}^-\) (oxidation number \(-1\)) while the rest is oxidized to \(\text{ClO}_3^-\), where chlorine is \(+5\). Because the same element (chlorine) is simultaneously oxidized and reduced, this is a disproportionation. (Choice B) is incorrect: this is a redox reaction, but two different elements change—aluminum is oxidized (\(0 \rightarrow +3\)) and bromine is reduced (\(0 \rightarrow -1\)); no single element is both oxidized and reduced, so it is not a disproportionation. (Choice C) is incorrect: this displacement reaction is redox, yet the changes occur in two separate elements—zinc is oxidized (\(0 \rightarrow +2\)) and copper is reduced (\(+2 \rightarrow 0\)); one element replacing another is not the same as one element splitting into two oxidation states. (Choice D) is incorrect: although one reactant breaks into two products, every element keeps its oxidation number (Ca is \(+2\), C is \(+4\), O is \(-2\) on both sides), so this is not even a redox reaction, let alone a disproportionation. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply oxidation-number assignments across each equation to determine which one has a single element that is both oxidized and reduced.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'd0736631-b2f1-4c15-aa31-51f2e34f4c29';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'd0736631-b2f1-4c15-aa31-51f2e34f4c29';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('d0736631-b2f1-4c15-aa31-51f2e34f4c29', 'B', 'misconception', 'treats any element-to-compound redox as disproportionation'),
  ('d0736631-b2f1-4c15-aa31-51f2e34f4c29', 'C', 'misconception', 'confuses single-displacement (two elements change) with disproportionation'),
  ('d0736631-b2f1-4c15-aa31-51f2e34f4c29', 'D', 'misconception', 'equates one-reactant-into-two-products with disproportionation, ignoring oxidation number');

-- [Types of Reactions] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'When chlorine gas is bubbled through cold dilute sodium hydroxide, the reaction proceeds as \(\text{Cl}_2 + 2\,\text{OH}^- \rightarrow \text{Cl}^- + \text{ClO}^- + \text{H}_2\text{O}\). Which statement correctly describes the oxidation-number changes in this reaction?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Chlorine is both oxidized and reduced, so it serves as both the oxidizing and reducing agent."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Chlorine is oxidized while oxygen is reduced, so oxygen serves as the oxidizing agent."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Chlorine is reduced while hydrogen is oxidized, so chlorine serves as the oxidizing agent."}, {"label": "D", "text": "No element changes oxidation number, so this reaction is not an oxidation-reduction process."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
